    {
      "text": "A nation should be defined by its shared language, history and culture rather than by descent or political citizenship.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Defining nationhood culturally, with language at the centre, is the core claim attributed to Herder and Fichte and to cultural nationalism generally.",
      "sources": [
        "s1",
        "s3"
      ],
      "addedAs": "preserve-national-culture"
    },
    {
      "text": "The state should support its national language, literature, arts and folk traditions.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Cultural nationalists work to sustain and develop the national culture, and the SEP says this can justify institutions that protect and develop it.",
      "sources": [
        "s2",
        "s3"
      ],
      "addedAs": "preserve-national-culture"
    },
    {
      "text": "Writers, scholars and artists have a leading role in forming a nation's identity.",
      "value": 2,
      "rationale": "Hutchinson and Smith describe intellectuals as central creators of national character and historical myth, and Herder collected popular songs to this end.",
      "sources": [
        "s1",
        "s3"
      ],
      "declined": "Role of intellectuals in forming national identity is a scholarly claim, not a contested political question."
    }
  ],
  "notes": "Strands differ: Fichte is more statist and wary of foreign influence, Herder more pluralist and cosmopolitan, and Hindutva-linked cultural nationalism adds religious positions that the general profile leaves at 0.",
